#
# Rotation for the log the fan controller writes. See newsyslog.conf(5).
#
# It is an ordinary file as well as syslog, and both on purpose: syslog is where an
# administrator's own tooling will find "the fans were handed back at 03:14 because the chipset
# sensor stopped answering", and the file is what the page tails and what a bug report is cut from.
# The controller is nearly silent - one line when it takes control, one when it gives it back, and
# one for every reason it ever refused - so a size limit rather than a schedule is right: a
# firewall that never has a problem never rotates this file at all, and one that is hunting will
# fill it in an afternoon.
#
# The signal goes to the daemon(8) supervisor named in the pidfile, not to the controller: daemon
# is what holds the log open, and it was started with -H so that SIGHUP means "close this file and
# open it again" and nothing else. The controller is not disturbed - which matters more here than
# it would elsewhere, because a signal it did not expect is a signal that ends with it handing the
# fans back and stopping, and log rotation is not a reason to do that.
#
# 600 is daemon(8)'s own mode for a file it creates, so the entry and the file agree on the first
# rotation as well as on the fiftieth.
#
# logfilename                    [owner:group]  mode  count  size  when  flags  [/pid_file]                         [sig_num]
/var/log/fanctl/fanctl.log       root:wheel     600   4      200   *     CJ     /var/run/fanctl/fanctl.supervisor.pid  1
